# scripts/pins.env - the SINGLE source of truth for every third-party artifact
# the release pipeline downloads.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (DRAGON-531): the pins used to live in each fetcher, and
# they drifted immediately. The Linux container built ffmpeg 8.0 while the macOS
# package bundled 8.1.2, so the same release shipped two different encoders on
# two platforms, and nothing but a human reading both files caught it. Every
# fetcher now reads THIS file, which makes "the same versions everywhere" true by
# construction instead of by vigilance.
#
# Read by:
#   scripts/fetch-mac-vendor.sh     (sources it directly)
#   scripts/fetch-win-vendor.ps1    (parses it into a hashtable)
#   .github/workflows/release.yml   (build-linux, the container ffmpeg build)
#   justfile                        (the linux `dist` recipe, same build)
#   scripts/appimage/Dockerfile     (the AppImage base image: ffmpeg, x264,
#                                    tesseract, the AppImage runtime)
#
# FORMAT: plain KEY=value, one per line, no spaces around `=`, no shell
# expansion. That is deliberate. bash can `source` it as-is, PowerShell parses it
# in three lines, and the container step needs no parser at all. TOML would buy
# nothing and cost a parser in bash.
#
# A value containing a SPACE must be double-quoted. Several are MIRROR LISTS,
# one space-separated URL per mirror, tried in order until one succeeds:
# `LIBPNG_URL`, `FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL`, `FFMPEG_SOURCE_ASC_URL`, `FFMPEG_GPG_KEY_URL`.
# bash `source` reads an unquoted `KEY=a b` as "set KEY=a, then run the command
# b", which is exactly the bug that broke the mac fetch once; the PowerShell
# parser strips the quotes to match.
#
# A mirror list is availability, never trust: every one of them is still gated by
# the same sha256 (and for ffmpeg, the same pinned GPG fingerprint), so a hostile
# mirror fails exactly as a hostile upstream would.
#
# CHANGING A PIN: update the version, the URL and the hash TOGETHER, and never
# add a hash you have not downloaded and computed yourself. Every fetcher treats
# a mismatch as fatal and never retries: the only two causes are an upstream file
# changing under a URL that promised to be immutable, or a pin edited without its
# hash, and neither gets better on a second download.
#
# NOTE ON REDUNDANCY: the version appears both as its own key and inside the
# URLs. That is on purpose, because the consumers use the version for the
# extracted directory name, so a URL and a version that disagree fail loudly at
# the `cd` rather than silently building the wrong thing.

# ── ffmpeg ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 9.0 (DRAGON-631), moved from 8.1.2 WITH the test pass the old pin's comment
# demanded: the full real-ffmpeg suite (`record::media_clock_e2e_tests`, all 15,
# plus `record::av_sync_tests`) ran green against ffmpeg 9.0 before this pin
# moved, and `ffmpeg-next` moved to 9.0.0 in the same change so the zero-copy
# bindings and the bundled libraries stay ONE generation. The ffmpeg-8 era
# defenses (MuxerWatchdog for the first-write wedge, the live `-itsoffset` ban
# for the ~200ms scheduler stall) were measured on 8 and STAY: they are bounded
# defenses, cheap when the bug is absent, and nobody has proven 9 free of them.
FFMPEG_VERSION=9.0

# Linux release artifact: the SOURCE tarball, built inside the Rocky 9 container
# only to supply the headers the zero-copy encoder compiles against.
#
# This is the ONLY artifact any upstream here signs, and it is verified by that
# GPG SIGNATURE rather than by checksum alone. The distinction is real: a hash we
# carry only proves the bytes still match what we saw when the pin was made,
# while the signature proves the FFmpeg project produced them. The fingerprint
# below is published on ffmpeg.org/download.html, so it is independently
# confirmable rather than trust-on-first-use, and the build requires the
# signature to be made by exactly it, so a compromised key URL supplying both a
# key and a matching signature still fails. The sha256 is a cheap first gate.
#
# MIRRORED, space-separated, tried in order, first success wins. Same shape as
# LIBPNG_URL below and consumed the same way.
#
# WHY (2026-08-06): ffmpeg.org took the v0.29.0 release build down. It was not
# even a clean outage, which would at least fail fast: DNS resolved, TCP 443
# opened, and TLS then reset the connection (`curl` exit 35), intermittently, so
# a plain retry could just as easily fail again. ffmpeg.org is the ONLY host
# these three files came from, and ALL FOUR Linux jobs build ffmpeg from source,
# so one flapping upstream took out every Linux artifact at once.
#
# The second URL is our own copy on the PUBLIC repo, tag `vendor-mirror`, a
# PRERELEASE so it can never become `releases/latest` and displace the manifest
# the in-app updater reads. GitHub release assets are immutable uploads, unlike a
# generated `/archive/` tarball whose bytes can change under a pinned hash (the
# reason X264_URL below takes Debian's pool rather than a GitLab archive).
#
# Mirroring adds AVAILABILITY, never trust: the sha256 gates the tarball and the
# signature must be by the pinned fingerprint, so a hostile mirror fails exactly
# as a hostile upstream would. Refresh the mirror whenever FFMPEG_VERSION moves,
# or the fallback silently stops matching and only helps until the primary breaks.
FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL="https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz https://github.com/Frosthaven/cosmic-capture-kit/releases/download/vendor-mirror/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz"
FFMPEG_SOURCE_ASC_URL="https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz.asc https://github.com/Frosthaven/cosmic-capture-kit/releases/download/vendor-mirror/ffmpeg-9.0.tar.xz.asc"
FFMPEG_SOURCE_SHA256=7f607a00dd0d28a729d5a4811205812eef01cf6ef6155025febb6f36a9062d52
FFMPEG_GPG_KEY_URL="https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-devel.asc https://github.com/Frosthaven/cosmic-capture-kit/releases/download/vendor-mirror/ffmpeg-devel.asc"
FFMPEG_GPG_FPR=FCF986EA15E6E293A5644F10B4322F04D67658D8

# macOS package: static arm64 binaries from martin-riedl.de, which keeps every
# release build at a permanent per-build URL and publishes a `.sha256` beside
# each artifact (the hashes below match those published files). No signature is
# offered, so a pinned hash is the honest best available here.
FFMPEG_MACOS_ARM64_BASE_URL=https://ffmpeg.martin-riedl.de/download/macos/arm64
FFMPEG_MACOS_ARM64_BUILD=1785863997_9.0
FFMPEG_MACOS_ARM64_SHA256=5267ef149ee0d208057a1b316aac079b661b0476574dee5da7d225769773c603
FFPROBE_MACOS_ARM64_SHA256=7778fbb533fb60d3336cbd9a9e51eced71658f020b570c7203590c1c41d42f50

# Windows package: the full_build-shared 9.0 from gyan.dev (DRAGON-631).
#
# WHY gyan.dev, after two years on BtbN: BtbN builds master plus the 8.1 and
# 7.1 release branches and, as of 2026-08-10, no 9.0 branch at all, so it had
# no 9.0 to pin. gyan.dev is the other builder ffmpeg.org itself links to,
# and it publishes every RELEASE at a permanent versioned URL under
# /packages/ with a `.sha256` beside it, which is a straight-up better
# durability story than the month-end-tag convention this pin used to ride on
# BtbN (whose daily tags are pruned). No signature is offered by either
# builder; a self-computed pinned hash that matches the published one is the
# honest best available, same as the macOS pin above.
#
# WHY SHARED rather than static: unchanged from the BtbN era. A static build
# compiles every codec library INTO each executable, so ffmpeg.exe,
# ffprobe.exe and ffplay.exe each carry their own copy of the same code; the
# shared build makes them small stubs beside one set of DLLs. The trade is
# one-sided because the Windows build is --no-default-features: it only ever
# SPAWNS ffmpeg.exe and never links libav, so this is purely a packaging-size
# decision, and the DLLs land exe-adjacent, where `util::locate_tool` looks.
#
# The archive's bin/ holds the three exes plus avcodec-63, avdevice-63,
# avfilter-12, avformat-63, avutil-61, swresample-7 and swscale-10 (the
# ffmpeg 9 generation, matching every other platform's bundle). Ship the
# WHOLE of bin/: cherry-picking the exes would produce three binaries that
# cannot start. It is a `.7z`, which costs nothing: the fetch script already
# hard-requires 7-Zip to unpack the tesseract installer.
FFMPEG_WINDOWS_X64_URL=https://www.gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/packages/ffmpeg-9.0-full_build-shared.7z
FFMPEG_WINDOWS_X64_SHA256=c07e3313aba524186c4684052e1c860dd2bb6983a7299668255939660126907a

# ── The Linux AppImage's own third-party parts (DRAGON-510) ───────────────────
#
# The AppImage bundles MORE third-party code than any other artifact, so it has
# more supply-chain exposure, not less. Everything below is downloaded by
# `scripts/appimage/Dockerfile` while it builds the base image and is verified
# before use.
#
# The AppImage's ffmpeg is built from the SAME `FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL` tarball above,
# with the same GPG check, so the bundled encoder is the pinned 8.1.2 and not a
# second ffmpeg from somewhere else. It is configured far wider than the headers
# build (x264, pulse, VAAPI, NVENC) because the AppImage ships the ffmpeg BINARY
# and its libav libraries, where the zip only needs headers.

# The AppImage runtime: the small static ELF that is concatenated in front of the
# squashfs payload and self-mounts it. Pinned to the DATED release tag, never
# `continuous`: `continuous` is a moving pointer whose asset is replaced in place,
# so it can never carry a checksum that stays true.
#
# STATIC-FUSE, which is the whole reason for taking this rather than a distro
# runtime: Debian 12 and Ubuntu 24.04 dropped libfuse2, and the usual fallback
# (`--appimage-extract-and-run`) unpacks the entire ~100 MB payload on EVERY
# launch. For a one-shot tool fired from PrintScreen that would be an extraction
# per screenshot.
#
# The release also publishes `.sig` files and its own `signing-pubkey.asc`. That
# is not a stronger check than the hash below: both the signature and the key
# come from the same release, so anyone who could replace the artifact could
# replace both. sha256 is the honest description of what we verify.
#
# ONE TAG, one runtime per architecture (DRAGON-529). Both are taken from the
# SAME release: the runtime is what mounts the payload and what sets `$APPIMAGE`,
# which the in-place self-update (DRAGON-532) depends on, so letting the two
# architectures drift to different runtime builds would mean debugging an update
# path that behaves differently per arch for no reason anyone wrote down.
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_TAG=20251108
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_URL=https://github.com/AppImage/type2-runtime/releases/download/20251108/runtime-x86_64
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_SHA256=2fca8b443c92510f1483a883f60061ad09b46b978b2631c807cd873a47ec260d
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_URL_AARCH64=https://github.com/AppImage/type2-runtime/releases/download/20251108/runtime-aarch64
APPIMAGE_RUNTIME_SHA256_AARCH64=00cbdfcf917cc6c0ff6d3347d59e0ca1f7f45a6df1a428a0d6d8a78664d87444

# x264, for the bundled ffmpeg's SOFTWARE H.264 encoder (`encode/plan.rs`'s
# software tier is literally `-c:v libx264`). Without it a machine with no VAAPI
# and no NVENC could not record at all, since ffmpeg has no native H.264 encoder.
#
# WHY DEBIAN'S TARBALL rather than videolan's: x264 has no releases and no
# tags. videolan's own snapshot directory stopped in 2019, and code.videolan.org's
# `/-/archive/<commit>` tarballs are GENERATED per request, so their bytes can
# change when GitLab changes its archiver, which would break a pinned hash for a
# reason that is not a supply-chain event. Debian's pool files are immutable by
# policy, and this one is a repack of the upstream git tree at 0480cb0
# (2025-09-10). The hash below is the gate either way.
X264_VERSION=0.165.3223+git20250910.0480cb0
X264_URL=https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/x/x264/x264_0.165.3223+git20250910.0480cb0.orig.tar.xz
X264_SHA256=de68ca43d0c6e09baeb86a38ed5cde86b0a84a21d6babcc569804201dc9df133

# nv-codec-headers: the header-only NVENC/NVDEC interface ffmpeg compiles
# against. It links NOTHING (ffmpeg dlopens `libnvidia-encode` at runtime), so
# this adds no dependency to the shipped binaries; it only decides whether
# `h264_nvenc` / `hevc_nvenc` exist in the bundled ffmpeg and libavcodec at all.
# They must, or every NVIDIA user of the AppImage silently drops to software
# encoding, including on the CUDA zero-copy path (`encode/cuda.rs`).
#
# 13.0.19.x is the series ffmpeg 8.1 requires. GitHub's `/archive/refs/tags/`
# tarballs are generated, like the GitLab ones rejected above; the difference is
# that no immutable alternative exists here, and `TESSERACT_SOURCE_URL` already
# accepts the same risk.
NVCODEC_VERSION=n13.0.19.1
NVCODEC_URL=https://github.com/FFmpeg/nv-codec-headers/archive/refs/tags/n13.0.19.1.tar.gz
NVCODEC_SHA256=c293161bd92dac53f2f8fab08abcd4f96773c3644a57b23c2260b4be2311ab2f

# ── tesseract (OCR) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Same version on both platforms so OCR behaves identically. macOS builds from
# source because no self-contained arm64 macOS tesseract exists to download;
# Windows takes the project's own official installer, published as a GitHub
# release asset. Neither carries a signature.
TESSERACT_VERSION=5.5.3
TESSERACT_SOURCE_URL=https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract/archive/refs/tags/5.5.3.tar.gz
TESSERACT_SOURCE_SHA256=9218e62793116d42a9f6d14cd9348518b27f382096eea3d0f2d1a24616bb5884
TESSERACT_WINDOWS_INSTALLER_URL=https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract/releases/download/5.5.3/tesseract-ocr-w64-setup-5.5.3.20260724.exe
TESSERACT_WINDOWS_INSTALLER_SHA256=bee9e3434bd94fd65387d9be28cd467a41f61b1275383b55b0f59a1331270ae4

# tesseract's dependencies, built statically into the macOS binary so the result
# links nothing outside the bundle. leptonica is built WITHOUT TIFF on purpose;
# scripts/fetch-mac-vendor.sh records why, including the upstream CMake bug that
# makes the alternative impossible.
LEPTONICA_VERSION=1.87.0
LEPTONICA_URL=https://github.com/DanBloomberg/leptonica/releases/download/1.87.0/leptonica-1.87.0.tar.gz
LEPTONICA_SHA256=c73363397f96eb1295602bf44d708a994ad42046c791bf03ea0505d829bdb6a7
LIBPNG_VERSION=1.6.50
# SEVERAL sources, tried in order until one yields the pinned bytes. SourceForge's
# `downloads.sourceforge.net` redirects to a geographically chosen mirror, and a
# dead mirror STALLS AT 0 B/s rather than erroring, which no amount of retrying
# fixes; it hung a mac build twice. The direct `dl.sourceforge.net` hosts skip
# that selection. Listing more than one is safe because the SHA-256 below is the
# gate: a host serving anything else fails as loudly as a single-URL mismatch.
LIBPNG_URL="https://deac-riga.dl.sourceforge.net/project/libpng/libpng16/1.6.50/libpng-1.6.50.tar.gz https://cytranet-dal.dl.sourceforge.net/project/libpng/libpng16/1.6.50/libpng-1.6.50.tar.gz https://downloads.sourceforge.net/project/libpng/libpng16/1.6.50/libpng-1.6.50.tar.gz"
LIBPNG_SHA256=708f4398f996325819936d447f982e0db90b6b8212b7507e7672ea232210949a

# The language pack, the SAME file and hash on both platforms so OCR results
# match. `tessdata_fast` is the LSTM-only, speed-tuned set, which is what a
# screenshot tool wants: `tessdata_best` is several times slower for accuracy
# that does not show up on UI text. Pinned to a tag, not `main`, so the file
# cannot change under the hash.
TESSDATA_TAG=4.1.0
TESSDATA_ENG_URL=https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tessdata_fast/raw/4.1.0/eng.traineddata
TESSDATA_ENG_SHA256=7d4322bd2a7749724879683fc3912cb542f19906c83bcc1a52132556427170b2

# ── proton-drive CLI (Proton Drive provider, DRAGON-566) ──────────────────────
#
# Proton's own official Drive command-line tool, which the Proton cloud
# provider drives as a child process (src/cloud/proton.rs). Only the FLATPAK
# bundles it: a sandboxed app cannot see a host install at all, so "put it on
# PATH" is advice that package makes false, and the MIT license pinned below is
# what makes redistributing a copy clean. The direct builds keep treating the
# tool as optional and external.
#
# The OFFICIAL prebuilt linux x86_64 binary, from Proton's own download host,
# which keeps every release at a permanent per-version URL. Proton publishes a
# SHA-512 beside each build; the sha256 below was computed from a download
# whose SHA-512 matched Proton's published value, so this pin is anchored to
# what Proton says it shipped. No signature is offered. A linux-arm64 build
# exists at the sibling path (linux-arm64/proton-drive); only x86_64 is pinned
# because the lab Flatpak is x86_64-only.
#
# Flathub follow-up: a submission-clean manifest would build the CLI from the
# ProtonDriveApps/sdk source (it is a Bun project) rather than pin the prebuilt
# binary. Prebuilt here keeps the lab loop short and tests the same bytes
# Proton ships.
#
# VERIFY BEFORE RE-PINNING, and NEVER STRIP: run the downloaded artifact's own
# `./proton-drive version` and require it to print
# `Proton Drive CLI cli-drive@<version>` with exit 0, where <version> matches
# PROTON_DRIVE_CLI_VERSION; a hash alone does not prove the file is the tool it
# claims to be. The binary is a Bun single-file executable (the bun runtime
# with the CLI's JS bundle appended to the ELF), so any ELF rewrite (eu-strip,
# objcopy, flatpak-builder's default debuginfo pass) severs the bundle and
# leaves a bare Bun runtime that self-reports BUN's version (1.3.14 at this
# pin) instead of the CLI's. That is how the fifth live test shipped a tool
# probing Broken while the pin itself was correct; the Flatpak module now sets
# no-debuginfo and the app module re-checks this sha after post-processing.
PROTON_DRIVE_CLI_VERSION=0.7.0
PROTON_DRIVE_CLI_URL=https://proton.me/download/drive/cli/0.7.0/linux-x64/proton-drive
PROTON_DRIVE_CLI_SHA256=4e3c74a7a25da00d7a0ca9ee223a9b7b0b238da357ab6fcfee04a5c313ddf4d1

# The tool's MIT license text, installed beside the bundled binary
# (/app/share/doc/proton-drive/LICENSE.md). Pinned at a COMMIT rather than a
# branch, so the bytes cannot change under the hash.
PROTON_DRIVE_LICENSE_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ProtonDriveApps/sdk/941aafa55fb179c8ee347c9d0b4660a3fc893f09/LICENSE.md
PROTON_DRIVE_LICENSE_SHA256=0ce1c7dc272b2b5f86de58045d87dbfdec582fdce352a4a41cc8c56bff51c6f5
